{:note "Calamaio's guides: the tutorial (C-h t), a buffer of its own you can practise in, and the tour, a read-only page of what the editor can do. Both are Calamaio's own text."
  :actions {:help-with-tutorial {:title Tutorial
      :category Help
      :description "Opens the tutorial in a buffer of its own, to read and practise in."
      :do [{:if "{{contains(keys(state.bufs), 'bt')}}" :then [{:run show :with {:b bt}} {:stop true}]}
        {:run listing-show :with (node #bt :buffer {:name TUTORIAL :mode text :text "{{computed.tutorialText}}"})}]}
    :show-tour {:title "Guided Tour"
      :category Help
      :description "Shows what Calamaio can do, with the keys that do it."
      :do {:run listing-show
        :with (node #bg :buffer {:name "*Tour*" :mode help :readOnly true :text "{{computed.tourText}}"})}}}
  :computed {:tutorialText "Calamaio tutorial.  This buffer is yours: try every key in it.\n\nC-x means: hold Control and press x.  M-x means: hold Alt (Option) and\npress x.  C-g cancels whatever you have begun.\n\n1. Moving\n   C-f forward a character     C-b back a character\n   C-n next line               C-p previous line\n   M-f forward a word          M-b back a word\n   C-a start of the line       C-e end of the line\n   M-< start of the buffer     M-> end of the buffer\n   C-v next screen             M-v previous screen\n   Try them now on these lines.\n\n2. A count\n   C-u 8 C-f moves eight characters; C-u alone means four.\n\n3. Killing and yanking\n   C-k kills the rest of a line; C-k again at the same place adds the\n   line break.  M-d kills a word.  C-SPC sets the mark: move, then C-w\n   kills the region between the mark and point, M-w copies it.\n   C-y yanks the last kill back; M-y right after swaps it for an older one.\n   Kill this line with C-k, then yank it below with C-n C-a C-y.\n\n4. Undo\n   C-/ undoes the last change, and C-M-/ redoes it.\n\n5. Files and buffers\n   C-x C-f visits a file, C-x C-s saves it, C-x C-w saves it under a new\n   name.  C-x b switches buffer, C-x C-b lists them, C-x k closes one.\n   C-x d lists a folder: RET on a line visits it.\n\n6. Windows\n   C-x 2 splits below, C-x 3 to the right, C-x o goes to the next window,\n   C-x 1 keeps only this one, C-x 0 removes this one.\n\n7. Searching\n   C-s searches forward as you type, C-r backward; RET stops at the\n   match, C-g goes back.  M-% replaces, asking at each match.\n\n8. Help\n   C-h k tells what a key does, C-h f what a command does, C-h b lists\n   every key, and M-x runs any command by name.\n\nThat is all you need to start.  C-x k closes this tutorial.\n"
    :tourText "A tour of Calamaio\n\nOne document.  Calamaio is a screen document: its keys, menus, commands,\ncolours and these words are JSON you can read and change while it runs.\n\nA keyboard first.  Every command has a name (M-x lists them) and most\nhave a key; C-h b shows them all, and C-h k tells what one does.\n\nBuffers and windows.  Each file gets a buffer; split the frame with C-x 2\nand C-x 3 to see several at once, and C-x b to put another in a window.\n\nThe kill ring.  What C-k, M-d, C-w and M-w take is kept, newest first:\nC-y brings it back and M-y goes further back in it.\n\nSearch as you type.  C-s and C-r find as you type, and M-% replaces\nwith a question at each match.\n\nColours.  Options > Load Theme switches between a light, a dark and a\nhigh-contrast theme; Options also turns line numbers, line wrapping and\nbracket matching on and off.\n\nFolders.  C-x d (or the folder on the tool bar) lists a folder; RET on a\nline visits that file.\n\nAn assistant.  C-c a (or the button at the bottom right) opens the\nassistant set up in Settings, to ask about what you are editing.\n\nq leaves this page.\n"}}
